  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Yak Butter Tea (Su You Cha)</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-500">The lifeblood of Tibetan culture. Salty, rich, energizing, and endlessly replenished in every household, tea house, and monastery across Shangri-La. An acquired taste that rewards the adventurous.</p>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What Is Yak Butter Tea?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Yak butter tea (u9165u6cb9u8336, su you cha) is the cornerstone of Tibetan cuisine and hospitality. The traditional recipe combines <strong>fermented dark tea leaves</strong> (often pu-erh), <strong>yak butter</strong> (rich, golden-yellow fat churned from yak milk), <strong>salt</strong>, and sometimes a splash of yak milk &mdash; all churned together in a wooden cylinder until emulsified into a smooth, creamy, slightly frothy liquid.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">For the uninitiated, the first sip can be surprising. It is <strong>salty, not sweet</strong>. The flavor is earthy, unctuous, and deeply savory &mdash; closer to a soup or broth than what most people think of as "tea." The mouthfeel is rich and coating from the yak butter fat. It is, unmistakably, an energy drink: a single cup contains 200&ndash;300 calories, making it essential fuel for life at 3,000+ meters where the body burns extra energy staying warm.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">In Tibetan culture, refusing yak butter tea is considered impolite. Hosts will continuously top up your bowl &mdash; if you've had enough, leave your bowl untouched and they will stop refilling. Draining it signals you want more.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Health Benefits</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Tibetans credit yak butter tea with several practical health benefits at altitude: the high fat content provides sustained energy and warmth in cold climates, the salt replenishes electrolytes lost through increased respiration in thin air, the antioxidants in fermented tea support digestion of the heavy meat-and-dairy Tibetan diet, and the combination helps prevent chapped lips &mdash; a genuine concern in the dry highland air. Scientific studies have noted the tea's high levels of conjugated linoleic acid (CLA) from yak butter, which has anti-inflammatory properties.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Try Yak Butter Tea in Shangri-La</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Traditional Tibetan Tea Houses (Best Experience)</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">In <strong>Dukezong Old Town</strong>, look for small, family-run tea houses with low wooden tables and yak-hair cushions. The tea house near Sifang Street Square and those along Pijiang Po lane are excellent. <strong>10&ndash;15 RMB</strong> per cup. These places are frequented by local Tibetans, offering the most authentic atmosphere. The tea is served in a thermos flask alongside small bowls.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Songzanlin Monastery Tea House</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">At the monastery exit, a simple tea house serves yak butter tea to pilgrims and visitors at <strong>10 RMB</strong>. The experience of sipping tea while watching monks walk past in maroon robes is unforgettable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Hotel &amp; Guesthouse Breakfast</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Most Tibetan-style guesthouses serve yak butter tea at breakfast, often included in the room rate. This is the easiest way to try it in a comfortable setting.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Buy Yak Butter &amp; Tea</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Yak butter</strong> (u7266u6cb9): Sold at the morning market near the east gate of Dukezong Old Town for <strong>30&ndash;50 RMB per 500g</strong>. Local farmers sell it wrapped in yak leather or cloth. It has a distinct golden color and rich aroma. In supermarkets in the new town, packaged yak butter is available for <strong>40&ndash;60 RMB per 500g</strong>.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Fermented tea bricks</strong>: Sold at tea shops throughout the old town. Pu-erh tea bricks suitable for butter tea cost <strong>20&ndash;60 RMB</strong>. A traditional wooden churn (dongmo) costs <strong>100&ndash;200 RMB</strong> as a souvenir &mdash; the tea shops on Sifang Street stock them.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Pre-made butter tea powder</strong> (instant): Available in most supermarkets for <strong>15&ndash;30 RMB per bag</strong>. Not as good as the real thing but convenient for travelers wanting to take the flavor home.</p>
  </section>

